1
00:00:01,715 --> 00:00:05,114
Why this lectures
on edge computing and cloud architecture.

2
00:00:05,609 --> 00:00:09,041
It's a hybrid,
a closed approach to modern applications

3
00:00:09,041 --> 00:00:12,308
instead of just putting everything
in the edge, everything in the cloud.

4
00:00:12,341 --> 00:00:13,925
We're going to talk
about a hybrid approach.

5
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:17,159
But before we do, let's talk
about some simple definitions.

6
00:00:17,192 --> 00:00:22,538
Cloud computing on demand highly scalable
typically public.

7
00:00:22,538 --> 00:00:24,947
But we need to talk public and private.

8
00:00:24,947 --> 00:00:27,785
It abstracts away physical infrastructure.

9
00:00:27,785 --> 00:00:31,547
So everything is, managed
through software defined infrastructure.

10
00:00:32,009 --> 00:00:34,286
Edge computing is almost the opposite.

11
00:00:34,286 --> 00:00:36,398
Everything's decentralized.

12
00:00:36,398 --> 00:00:37,949
I know where nodes are.

13
00:00:37,949 --> 00:00:40,787
They have meaning,
when they're out there,

14
00:00:40,787 --> 00:00:43,955
they could be mobile, 
edge nodes, fixed edge nodes.

15
00:00:43,955 --> 00:00:45,506
There's lots of things out there.

16
00:00:45,506 --> 00:00:50,060
The goal with edge computing and pushing
compute out to the edge is minimizing

17
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:56,264
latency, saving bandwidth and decreasing
costs and increasing reliability.

18
00:00:56,264 --> 00:00:57,716
If we can.

19
00:00:57,716 --> 00:00:58,409
Right.

20
00:00:58,409 --> 00:01:01,412
Motivation for hybrid architectures.

21
00:01:01,478 --> 00:01:05,834
I want to, because
we've had such a big explosive growth in,

22
00:01:06,362 --> 00:01:10,058
IoT and edge sensors and edge computing,

23
00:01:10,322 --> 00:01:13,523
we need some way of managing all this data
that's being generated.

24
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:16,493
And there is a lot of data
that's being generated.

25
00:01:16,493 --> 00:01:19,793
So why not do some of the work on the edge

26
00:01:19,793 --> 00:01:23,852
where the data is
and only pass up into the cloud?

27
00:01:23,852 --> 00:01:28,109
The data that's required to do aggregation
or to do higher level

28
00:01:28,109 --> 00:01:29,957
analytics that I need to do.

29
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:34,115
I this is a balancing point
that I have between regulatory

30
00:01:34,676 --> 00:01:37,547
and cost and benefits.

31
00:01:37,547 --> 00:01:40,781
So a lot of times
this is not a technical decision

32
00:01:40,781 --> 00:01:44,444
as much as it is
a business decision that I have to make.

33
00:01:44,609 --> 00:01:47,282
So these are a lot of things
that you have to weigh as an architect.

34
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:49,031
All right.

35
00:01:49,031 --> 00:01:52,298
Let's talk about just high level
view of the cloud architecture.

36
00:01:52,958 --> 00:01:54,806
Very simple. My physical layer.

37
00:01:54,806 --> 00:02:00,185
That's obviously the physical things
I can touch in the data center racks, data

38
00:02:00,185 --> 00:02:04,079
centers, network equipment, compute nodes,
storage nodes, all that stuff.

39
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:07,181
Virtualization layer,
all the cloud service providers

40
00:02:07,181 --> 00:02:11,405
use different virtualization layers,
whether they're hypervisors for VMs

41
00:02:12,197 --> 00:02:14,441
or their container,

42
00:02:14,441 --> 00:02:17,510
clouds
that are out there that are using Docker

43
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:20,513
or one of the other, systems
that are out there.

44
00:02:20,909 --> 00:02:24,737
Then on top of that virtualization layer,
I have a management orchestration layer

45
00:02:25,034 --> 00:02:28,499
that is managing that software
defined infrastructure,

46
00:02:28,499 --> 00:02:31,634
spinning up storage network, compute.

47
00:02:32,690 --> 00:02:35,264
That's the management orchestration layer.

48
00:02:35,264 --> 00:02:38,630
And then I have my middle, 
middleware services

49
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:43,316
where I've got databases and message
busses and, you know, all the things

50
00:02:43,316 --> 00:02:48,728
and monitoring I did identity management
systems, all the things that are there

51
00:02:48,728 --> 00:02:52,754
to support the top layer,
which is the application layer, right?

52
00:02:53,117 --> 00:02:54,173
The application layer.

53
00:02:54,173 --> 00:02:56,450
The job there is
that's where I'm writing my code.

54
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:59,684
And I'm taking advantage
of the middleware, the orchestration,

55
00:02:59,684 --> 00:03:02,456
the virtualization layer
in the physical layer. Right.

56
00:03:02,456 --> 00:03:04,238
So that's the idea behind cloud.

57
00:03:04,238 --> 00:03:06,581
I only focus on the application layer.

58
00:03:06,581 --> 00:03:08,627
All the other stuff is handled for me
in the cloud.

59
00:03:08,627 --> 00:03:09,749
That's the idea behind it.

60
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:13,115
Huge benefits in cloud.

61
00:03:13,115 --> 00:03:15,194
We've talked about this in other lectures.

62
00:03:15,194 --> 00:03:18,197
Elasticity, global distribution.

63
00:03:18,296 --> 00:03:19,814
Managed services.

64
00:03:19,814 --> 00:03:21,365
I don't have to worry about them.

65
00:03:21,365 --> 00:03:24,797
I can pay as I go
so I can spin up a thousand machines

66
00:03:24,797 --> 00:03:25,853
and tear them down,

67
00:03:25,853 --> 00:03:29,120
and I only pay for the time I use
instead of having a thousand machines

68
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:33,872
sitting in my data center that I spend
a lot of capital expenditures on, right?

69
00:03:34,268 --> 00:03:37,865
Common challenges
that we have with cloud latency

70
00:03:37,865 --> 00:03:41,528
sometimes, user
experience can be kind of there

71
00:03:41,528 --> 00:03:45,620
if I've got bad latency,
egress, bandwidth costs.

72
00:03:45,917 --> 00:03:48,392
Moving data out of the cloud is expensive.

73
00:03:48,392 --> 00:03:51,494
We've got regional outages
that can cause problems.

74
00:03:51,791 --> 00:03:53,870
And then sometimes
I have some compliance issues

75
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:56,279
where where the data is actually
stored really matters.

76
00:03:57,797 --> 00:03:59,150
Edge computing.

77
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:03,605
Let's talk about edge,
drivers behind edge, ultra low latency.

78
00:04:03,605 --> 00:04:07,400
If I need to have a, read a sensor

79
00:04:07,796 --> 00:04:11,921
and I need it to react immediately,
let's say at a nuclear power plant,

80
00:04:12,251 --> 00:04:16,013
I don't know that I want, all my data
going up to a cloud

81
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:19,148
to be analyzed
before I turn off a nuclear reactor.

82
00:04:19,478 --> 00:04:21,821
I want that stuff, you know, close, close.

83
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:27,233
And so the key drivers there are ultra low
latency requirements, bandwidth savings.

84
00:04:27,233 --> 00:04:32,513
If I'm generating a lot of data
instead of, processing out in the cloud,

85
00:04:32,513 --> 00:04:36,935
maybe I process part of it, locally first
and then send stuff up to the cloud.

86
00:04:37,331 --> 00:04:40,796
And if I'm worried about data privacy
and local processing, things like that,

87
00:04:40,796 --> 00:04:44,855
those are some things that I could most
definitely do at the edge.

88
00:04:45,614 --> 00:04:48,617
There are lots of different types
of, edge nodes out there.

89
00:04:49,013 --> 00:04:53,204
Device nodes, like phones and cameras,
wearables, things like that.

90
00:04:53,732 --> 00:04:59,177
Even a laptop is a device edge
or I have got local edge.

91
00:04:59,177 --> 00:05:04,787
This could be a micro data center
or a gateway or an edge appliance.

92
00:05:04,787 --> 00:05:09,275
That's sitting there, at a location
I can have regional edge

93
00:05:09,275 --> 00:05:12,311
devices, where, maybe in a factory.

94
00:05:12,311 --> 00:05:16,205
I have, multiple buildings in a factory.

95
00:05:16,568 --> 00:05:20,264
And the factory is a regional edge
and the local edge of the,

96
00:05:20,264 --> 00:05:22,574
in each building type of thing.

97
00:05:22,574 --> 00:05:27,920
So you have this aggregation of, of edge,
and then I've got, the cloud

98
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:32,077
service providers know that, hey,
I want to be part of this edge revolution.

99
00:05:32,374 --> 00:05:35,377
So they've created
these cloud edge services

100
00:05:35,476 --> 00:05:38,545
that allow me to start moving data
into the edge.

101
00:05:38,545 --> 00:05:40,987
And these architectures
that are built around that.

102
00:05:43,033 --> 00:05:46,531
Edge architectures
are a little bit different than cloud,

103
00:05:46,531 --> 00:05:50,194
because cloud is all about unification
and putting everything,

104
00:05:50,524 --> 00:05:53,725
more homogeneous, even though I may have

105
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:56,827
heterogeneous background, hardware,

106
00:05:57,058 --> 00:06:00,853
I want as much homogeneity
as possible in the edge.

107
00:06:00,853 --> 00:06:02,272
I'm dealing with heterogeneous.

108
00:06:02,272 --> 00:06:05,374
I'm dealing with disconnected part time
running things.

109
00:06:05,374 --> 00:06:08,311
It's so the architecture
is a little bit different,

110
00:06:08,311 --> 00:06:10,225
but there's a lot of similarity.

111
00:06:10,225 --> 00:06:14,515
And having a control plane
and a workload manager type of thing.

112
00:06:14,878 --> 00:06:18,739
One of the key differences in edge is
I have to deal with data

113
00:06:18,739 --> 00:06:21,808
a lot more effectively than in the cloud.

114
00:06:21,808 --> 00:06:26,131
And the cloud storage
is the big massive storage, right?

115
00:06:26,131 --> 00:06:28,474
It's accessible everywhere on the edge.

116
00:06:28,474 --> 00:06:29,365
That's not the case.

117
00:06:29,365 --> 00:06:33,127
And I don't necessarily want all my edge
data available everywhere.

118
00:06:33,358 --> 00:06:35,668
So your data plane becomes important.

119
00:06:35,668 --> 00:06:39,595
Typically you'll have an edge
agent running on the individual edge,

120
00:06:39,595 --> 00:06:43,819
giving me health metrics and runtime
statistics and things like that.

121
00:06:44,809 --> 00:06:45,733
Also, with the edge,

122
00:06:45,733 --> 00:06:48,736
you have to be concerned
about physical security

123
00:06:48,736 --> 00:06:52,630
as well as, logical or cyber security.

124
00:06:53,026 --> 00:06:53,752
Right.

125
00:06:53,752 --> 00:06:55,600
Your data is out there.

126
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:58,603
It's close to where people can touch it.

127
00:06:59,098 --> 00:07:02,101
So encryption,
local encryption is important.

128
00:07:02,266 --> 00:07:05,137
Leveraging TPMs for secure elements,

129
00:07:05,137 --> 00:07:08,767
secure boot, firmware verification,
validation, things like that.

130
00:07:08,899 --> 00:07:12,661
So managing those edge devices
ends up being, extremely important.

131
00:07:13,519 --> 00:07:15,664
If I start looking at,

132
00:07:15,664 --> 00:07:18,931
hybrid architectures,
cloud edge architectures,

133
00:07:19,888 --> 00:07:24,838
there's some similarities in,
the management plane of microservices.

134
00:07:24,838 --> 00:07:27,313
For example,
if I've developed those correctly,

135
00:07:27,313 --> 00:07:28,237
I can take advantage

136
00:07:28,237 --> 00:07:31,240
of those microservices
and they can easily move between things.

137
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:36,817
But I have to have some common
functionality across my edge and my core,

138
00:07:37,147 --> 00:07:41,041
and that's that orchestration layer
and a virtualization layer.

139
00:07:41,503 --> 00:07:41,701
All right.

140
00:07:41,701 --> 00:07:45,562
So having those figured out
and having a match

141
00:07:45,562 --> 00:07:48,136
between cloud and edge become important.

142
00:07:48,136 --> 00:07:51,502
The other interesting thing
that you have to deal with

143
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:54,868
is data management across across all this.

144
00:07:55,165 --> 00:07:57,211
So it's a little bit different

145
00:07:57,211 --> 00:08:01,303
when you're talking these hybrid
because now I'm managing do I move data.

146
00:08:01,369 --> 00:08:04,372
Do I move applications. How do I do it.

147
00:08:04,603 --> 00:08:08,596
We see a lot more message bus passing
between this

148
00:08:08,893 --> 00:08:11,764
as the number of nodes increases,

149
00:08:11,764 --> 00:08:15,724
as the complexity can increase without
putting something like a message bus,

150
00:08:15,724 --> 00:08:20,872
like a pub StubHub, like Kafka or Pulsar
or Rabbit MQ in in the middle there.

151
00:08:20,872 --> 00:08:25,789
So I think the integration patterns
are different because I'm dealing with

152
00:08:25,987 --> 00:08:30,937
tens of thousands of heterogeneous nodes
that can drop in and out continuously.

153
00:08:30,937 --> 00:08:34,171
So understanding those design patterns
ends up being important.

154
00:08:35,557 --> 00:08:38,593
Lots of really cool use cases out there,

155
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:45,325
that continue to grow like, AR and VR,
where I need latency.

156
00:08:45,325 --> 00:08:49,087
That would be horrible
if I had a screen on my eyes

157
00:08:49,087 --> 00:08:52,057
and I was waiting on the
on the cloud service providers.

158
00:08:52,849 --> 00:08:54,829
Because that computes so far away.

159
00:08:54,829 --> 00:08:57,106
Let's put the compute
right in the goggles,

160
00:08:57,106 --> 00:09:01,264
and do some edge, rendering,
instead of waiting.

161
00:09:01,264 --> 00:09:03,508
And then push updates up to the cloud.
Right.

162
00:09:03,508 --> 00:09:07,435
So architecting what you put in the cloud
and what you keep on the edges

163
00:09:07,435 --> 00:09:08,689
is really important.

164
00:09:08,689 --> 00:09:10,867
Health care monitoring a big deal.

165
00:09:10,867 --> 00:09:15,091
We see this with smart beds and wearables
and things like that.

166
00:09:15,256 --> 00:09:18,523
I'm doing a lot of processing on the edge,
and I'm always sending up,

167
00:09:18,589 --> 00:09:21,592
things into the cloud as needed.

168
00:09:21,757 --> 00:09:24,265
Right. Retail and smart buildings.

169
00:09:24,265 --> 00:09:30,073
Another great, edge to cloud case
where I can have a building.

170
00:09:30,073 --> 00:09:34,297
I can have a set of buildings on a campus,
I can run aggregation nodes,

171
00:09:34,561 --> 00:09:39,412
I can have a whole company
with everything managed, locally.

172
00:09:39,412 --> 00:09:41,095
But also up in the cloud.

173
00:09:41,095 --> 00:09:45,055
So I do some things local
and some things up in the cloud, where

174
00:09:45,055 --> 00:09:48,223
I get a big picture view of everything,
but I can make things happen.

175
00:09:48,850 --> 00:09:50,896
Locally autonomous vehicles.

176
00:09:50,896 --> 00:09:51,787
Can you imagine

177
00:09:52,876 --> 00:09:54,097
autonomous vehicles that

178
00:09:54,097 --> 00:09:57,166
