179
00:09:57,298 --> 00:09:59,311
We'd have accidents all over the place.

180
00:09:59,311 --> 00:10:00,697
Latency problems.

181
00:10:00,697 --> 00:10:05,383
So really, we need some control
in, in the car itself.

182
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:09,706
And then,
you know, obviously pushing up telemetry.

183
00:10:10,003 --> 00:10:14,854
Specific telemetry up into the cloud
could give me a bigger picture

184
00:10:14,854 --> 00:10:18,154
on, you know, what's going on
with all the vehicles in my fleet.

185
00:10:18,154 --> 00:10:21,223
So understanding
where your data is, where it needs to be

186
00:10:21,223 --> 00:10:24,622
and where decisions are made
and where they need to react to,

187
00:10:24,655 --> 00:10:27,988
is key to developing,
these types of architectures.

188
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:30,265
Okay.

189
00:10:30,265 --> 00:10:32,872
So because the

190
00:10:32,872 --> 00:10:36,667
because there isn't a one design pattern
that fits everything

191
00:10:37,327 --> 00:10:40,957
coming up with your architecture,
you should be leaning

192
00:10:40,957 --> 00:10:43,927
on a lot of the design patterns
for distributed computing

193
00:10:44,389 --> 00:10:49,207
and understand all the key factors
in making those design decisions.

194
00:10:49,537 --> 00:10:52,507
Resource
constraints are a big deal, right?

195
00:10:52,705 --> 00:10:55,048
How much do I have?

196
00:10:55,048 --> 00:10:57,853
How much resource do I have at the edge?

197
00:10:57,853 --> 00:11:01,614
Do I have, a specialized accelerators

198
00:11:01,614 --> 00:11:04,617
that can handle the type of workload
I have at the edge?

199
00:11:05,079 --> 00:11:08,544
If the answer is no, then
maybe I need to go up in the cloud, right?

200
00:11:08,544 --> 00:11:10,326
Or you got to look at cost.

201
00:11:10,326 --> 00:11:13,230
How much does it cost to put
that specialized,

202
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:16,464
acceleration in the edge
if it's cost prohibitive?

203
00:11:16,464 --> 00:11:19,698
So there's a lot of things you've got,
you got to weigh here, right?

204
00:11:20,226 --> 00:11:25,341
Also use minimal base images
as much as you can on the edge.

205
00:11:25,341 --> 00:11:27,222
Also in the cloud too, right.

206
00:11:27,222 --> 00:11:30,621
If you're using containers,
use alpine distros.

207
00:11:31,083 --> 00:11:33,987
They're very small, lightweight
and very secure.

208
00:11:33,987 --> 00:11:35,142
That's the idea.

209
00:11:35,142 --> 00:11:38,145
Decrease your attack footprint.

210
00:11:38,277 --> 00:11:43,326
Also look at ways
that you can use, automation

211
00:11:43,590 --> 00:11:47,253
for deployment
and management of these solutions.

212
00:11:47,517 --> 00:11:50,256
They can be very tough to manage.

213
00:11:50,256 --> 00:11:53,688
Because you are talking about distributing
across thousands of nodes.

214
00:11:54,117 --> 00:11:57,978
There a lot of time there isn't one pane
of glass that you can do this through.

215
00:11:58,407 --> 00:12:01,344
There are some frameworks
that are starting to emerge

216
00:12:01,344 --> 00:12:05,601
that can help with that,
but you still need a way of distributing

217
00:12:05,832 --> 00:12:09,066
and use, best practices in DevOps around,

218
00:12:09,462 --> 00:12:12,432
you know, rolling
updates and things like that.

219
00:12:14,643 --> 00:12:15,567
Make sure that you

220
00:12:15,567 --> 00:12:19,032
look at security and compliance,
especially with the edge devices.

221
00:12:19,461 --> 00:12:23,322
Make sure you have hardware root of trust
so I can trust where I'm getting data

222
00:12:23,322 --> 00:12:27,249
from, where I'm pushing data to out
in, in the edge.

223
00:12:27,777 --> 00:12:30,384
Make sure that you do have,
policy engines

224
00:12:30,384 --> 00:12:34,740
that can, to local reinforcement of,
policies,

225
00:12:34,938 --> 00:12:38,832
add in the edge device
itself, use as many open standards

226
00:12:38,832 --> 00:12:42,594
as you can to break down
some of that heterogeneity problem.

227
00:12:42,759 --> 00:12:44,706
So I can have better protocols.

228
00:12:44,706 --> 00:12:48,930
I can have consistent protocols
coming back and doing analytics.

229
00:12:51,207 --> 00:12:51,867
All right.

230
00:12:51,867 --> 00:12:55,332
Some really interesting
things that we're starting to see,

231
00:12:55,695 --> 00:12:58,335
to push machine
learning down into the edge.

232
00:12:58,335 --> 00:13:02,097
We're having TensorFlow Lite
